1
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:22,000
A homespun murder story.

2
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Fargo is a crime, drama, thriller film directed by Joel Coen.

3
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:54,000
Jerry, a small-town Minnesota car salesman is bursting at the seams with debt...

4
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:10,000
but he's got a plan.

5
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:26,000
He's going to hire two thugs to kidnap his wife in a scheme to
collect a hefty ransom from his wealthy father-in-law.

6
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:42,000
It's going to be a snap and nobody's going to get hurt...

7
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:58,000
until people start dying.

8
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:14,000
Enter Police Chief Marge, a coffee-drinking, parka-wearing - and extremely
pregnant - investigator who'll stop at nothing to get her man.

9
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:30,000
And if you think her small-time investigative skills will
give the crooks a run for their ransom...

10
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,000
you betcha!

11
00:35:56,000 --> 00:36:02,000
Marge Gunderson, played by Frances McDormand.

12
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:18,000
Jerry Lundegaard, played by William H. Macy.

13
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:34,000
Carl Showalter, played by Steve Buscemi.

14
00:45:44,000 --> 00:45:50,000
Gaear Grimsrud, played by Peter Stormare.

15
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:06,000
Wade Gustafson, played by Harve Presnell.

16
00:52:16,000 --> 00:52:22,000
Norm Gunderson, played by John Carroll Lynch.

17
00:55:32,000 --> 00:55:38,000
Fargo is a 1996 black comedy and crime drama film written,
directed, produced and edited by Joel and Ethan Coen.

18
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:54,000
It stars Frances McDormand, William H.

19
01:02:04,000 --> 01:02:10,000
Macy, Steve Buscemi, Harve Presnell, and Peter Stormare.

20
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:26,000
McDormand plays police chief Marge Gunderson, who investigates after a car salesman, Jerry Lundegaard (Macy),
hires two dimwitted criminals to kidnap his wife to extort a ransom from her wealthy father.

21
01:08:36,000 --> 01:08:42,000
Filmed in the United States in late 1995, Fargo premiered at the 1996
Cannes Film Festival, where it competed for the Palme d'Or.

22
01:11:52,000 --> 01:11:58,000
Joel Coen won the festival's Prix De La Mise En Scène (Best Director Award).

23
01:15:08,000 --> 01:15:14,000
The film was a critical and commercial success, earning particular acclaim for the Coens'
direction and script and the performances of McDormand, Macy and Buscemi.

24
01:18:24,000 --> 01:18:30,000
Fargo received seven Oscar nominations at the 69th Academy Awards, including Best Picture, Best Director, and Best
Supporting Actor for Macy, winning two: Best Actress for McDormand and Best Original Screenplay for the Coens.

25
01:21:40,000 --> 01:21:46,000
In 1998, the American Film Institute named Fargo one of the 100 greatest American films,
the most recent film on the list at point, but it was delisted in 2007.

26
01:24:56,000 --> 01:25:02,000
In 2006, it was selected for preservation in the United States National Film Registry by the
Library of Congress as "culturally, historically, or aesthetically significant".

27
01:28:12,000 --> 01:28:18,000
An FX television series based on the film, Fargo, premiered in 2014.

28
01:31:28,000 --> 01:31:34,000
The film explores themes of salesclerk, unsociability, police, cheating, ransom.

29
01:34:44,000 --> 01:34:50,000
The tone is intense and dark and thought_provoking.
